<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Angad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1504">1504</a>–<a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Lehna, Lahina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Angad was the second of the ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Sikh gurus</a>. Born Lehna, a Khatri and a worshipper of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> at Kartarpur, served him there, and was named Angad, &quot;my own limb&quot;, as successor in 1539. After Nanak's death on 22 September 1539 he led the community from Khadur Sahib, though few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> at first accepted him because Nanak's sons also claimed the succession. He is remembered for formalising the Gurmukhi alphabet, although scholars note the script pre-dates Sikhism, and for systematising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langar-sikhism">langar</a>. He contributed 62 or 63 saloks and chose Amar Das, not his own son, as successor. He died on 29 March 1552.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-angad.jpg" alt="Guru Angad" width="330" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: possibly the family workshop (or atelier) of Nainsukh of Guler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Angad_miniature_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Guru Angad</b> developed the Gurmukhi script in which the Sikh scriptures are written.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>His successor, <b>Guru Angad</b>, opened centres and distributed the hymns, and the community sang them while his agents collected donations.</blockquote><a href="/work/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It is mostly the writing of Guru Nanak and was put together by <b>Guru Angad</b>: it begins with the Mool Mantra, continues with 38 paudis (stanzas) in different poetic metres, and ends with a Salok by Guru Angad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li></ul></section>
